\section{Methods and Experimental Setup}
\label{sec:experimental_setup}

\subsection{Architectures and Benchmark}
\label{sec:setup_overview}

Seven VLA architectures spanning three vision connector families provide the testbed for our pruning study (Figure~\ref{fig:architecture}).

\textbf{Linear projection.} $\pi_0$~\cite{black2024pi0} (3.3B parameters, 256 tokens) maps SigLIP~\cite{zhai2023siglip} patches through a linear layer into a PaliGemma backbone and generates actions via flow matching~\cite{lipman2023flow}. $\pi_0.5$~\cite{black2025pi05} (4B parameters, 768 vision tokens from three cameras: left wrist, right wrist, base) applies a linear projection with $\times\!\sqrt{d}$ scaling into the same PaliGemma backbone with flow matching action generation. X-VLA~\cite{zheng2025xvla} (880M parameters, 50 tokens) augments its linear projection with a 12-layer BART encoder~\cite{lewis2020bart}; self-attention in these layers introduces cross-token mixing that makes the effective pipeline more complex than the nominal ``linear projection'' label suggests.

\textbf{MLP projector.} OpenVLA~\cite{kim2024openvla} (7B parameters, 256 tokens) fuses DINOv2~\cite{oquab2023dinov2} and SigLIP encoders through a three-layer MLP (GELU activations) that maps each patch independently into a Llama-2 backbone. OFT~\cite{kim2025openoft} shares this MLP connector with an optimized fine-tuning action head; we include OFT for attention entropy comparison only, as both metrics match OpenVLA and confirm the connector determines token importance structure. CogACT~\cite{li2024cogact} uses an MLP projector and retains 74.2\% SR under 78\% pruning as reported by EfficientVLA~\cite{yang2025efficientvla}; we cite this result as literature corroboration of the graded response pattern rather than from our own evaluation.

\textbf{Spatial folding.} SmolVLA~\cite{shukor2025smolvla} (450M parameters, 128 tokens from two cameras) encodes each camera image with SigLIP into 1024 patches, then applies a pixel-shuffle connector that folds each $4{\times}4$ block into one token by channel concatenation (64 per camera). Each token encodes 16 spatially contiguous patches; removing one eliminates an entire $4{\times}4$ receptive field. The tokens enter a Qwen2-0.5B backbone~\cite{yang2024qwen2} with flow matching action generation.

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\linewidth]{figures/paper/fig_architecture.pdf}
\caption{Vision connector comparison. SmolVLA's pixel-shuffle folds $4{\times}4$ patches into single tokens (128 total, spatially entangled). OpenVLA's MLP projector maps each patch independently (256 tokens, per-token independence).}
\label{fig:architecture}
\end{figure}

We evaluate on LIBERO~\cite{liu2023libero}, a suite of language-conditioned tabletop manipulation tasks organized into three suites: Object (10 tasks, 50 episodes each, 500 total), Spatial (10 tasks), and Goal (10 tasks). Object serves as the primary evaluation; Spatial and Goal provide cross-suite validation. Closed-loop success rate (SR) with Wilson 95\% confidence intervals is the primary metric. Full fine-tuning baselines on Object: SmolVLA 44.8\%, OpenVLA 74.0\%, X-VLA 99.2\%, $\pi_0.5$ 98.2\%.

\subsection{Pruning Protocol}
\label{sec:pruning_protocol}

Two complementary pruning methods bracket each model's robustness from above and below.

\textbf{L2 norm-based removal} (importance scoring) ranks vision tokens by their L2 norm and physically removes the lowest-norm fraction. Norm ranking preserves the most informative tokens by construction, so L2 removal provides a more favorable test of pruning tolerance.

\textbf{Random removal} (uniform selection) selects tokens uniformly at random and physically removes the unselected ones. Random removal provides a conservative lower bound because it makes no attempt to preserve high-importance tokens.

Model-specific retention ratios reflect each architecture's token count and expected sensitivity. $\pi_0$ is evaluated offline only ($k{=}0.1$, normalized L1 action loss) because its deployment environment precludes closed-loop testing. $\pi_0.5$ uses $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7\}$ under both L2 and random removal in closed-loop evaluation. X-VLA spans $k \in \{0.1, 0.2, 0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.8, 0.85, 0.9, 0.95\}$ with L2 removal across three seeds, providing the finest-grained retention sweep in our study. OpenVLA uses $k \in \{0.3, 0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$ with random removal as the primary protocol and L2 for direct comparison. SmolVLA tests seven configurations varying scoring (learned ACIS vs.\ random), retention ratio ($k \in \{0.5, 0.7, 0.9, 0.95\}$), and mechanism (zero-masking, true removal, token merging via ToMe~\cite{bolya2023tome}).

The $\pi_0$--OpenVLA pair forms a controlled natural experiment: both consume 256 SigLIP tokens from the same encoder lineage, differing only in their connector (linear projection vs.\ three-layer MLP). Any performance gap at matched retention ratios isolates the connector as the causal factor rather than encoder capacity or token count.

\subsection{Vision Token Re-encoder (VTR)}
\label{sec:smart_compress_setup}

A cross-attention pooling module (22.2M parameters, $\sim$5\% of SmolVLA) is inserted between the pixel-shuffle connector and the LLM backbone. $M$ learned queries attend over all 128 source tokens, producing $M$ compressed representations. The compression sweep covers $M \in \{16, 32, 64, 96, 128\}$, where $M{=}128$ serves as a passthrough control that adds parameters without reducing tokens.

A parameter-matched MLP adapter (22.14M parameters) serves as an architecture control, testing whether the benefit comes from cross-attention specifically or from re-encoding in general. Both VTR and the baseline train from the same SmolVLA-Base checkpoint for 100K steps with identical hyperparameters. An extended training control (same procedure without the compressor) achieves 45.0\% SR, statistically indistinguishable from the 44.8\% baseline ($p > 0.9$); any gain from VTR is therefore architectural, not from additional optimization steps. Training details appear in Appendix~\ref{app:smart_compress_details}.

\subsection{Diagnostic Metrics}
\label{sec:diagnostic_metrics}

Two metrics computed from a single forward pass on task-relevant frames characterize each model's vision token structure at zero additional cost.

\textbf{Token importance CV} is the coefficient of variation of L2 norms across all vision tokens. High CV signals a heterogeneous importance distribution where some tokens carry substantially more information than others; low CV indicates near-uniform norms. CV tracks pruning tolerance continuously across the seven-model taxonomy (Section~\ref{sec:taxonomy_overview}): uniformly critical models cluster at CV $< 0.06$, threshold-graded at $\sim$0.15, smooth graded at $\sim$0.22, and catastrophic cliff at $> 0.3$.

\textbf{Attention entropy ratio} is the normalized Shannon entropy of attention weights over vision tokens. Low entropy indicates concentrated attention on a few tokens, while high entropy reflects diffuse attention across the full token set. CV measures the input-side importance gradient; entropy captures how the downstream LLM allocates attention, providing a complementary view of the same structural property.

